  <h2>2. Quotations and how a contract is formed</h2>
  <p>
    An enquiry through this site, by telephone, email or WhatsApp is not a booking. We will give you a written
    quotation covering the specific vehicle, route and service. A quotation is valid for <strong>14 days</strong>
    unless it says otherwise, and remains subject to space being available on the sailing.
  </p>
  <p>
    A contract comes into effect when you accept our quotation in writing and we confirm the booking. At that
    point these terms and the quotation together form the agreement between us. Anything discussed but not
    written into the quotation is not part of it.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Prices and payment</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Prices are quoted in pounds sterling unless we agree otherwise in writing.</li>
    <li>
      Payment is required in cleared funds before the vehicle is loaded, unless the quotation sets out different
      terms. We do not release a vehicle or the export documents against an unpaid invoice.
    </li>
    <li>
      Where a vehicle qualifies for VAT-free supply or a VAT refund on export, that treatment depends on the
      conditions HMRC sets, including evidence that the vehicle left the UK within the required period. If those
      conditions are not met, the VAT becomes payable and you are responsible for it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Freight rates, bunker surcharges and port charges are set by carriers and terminals, not by us. If they
      change between quotation and loading, we will tell you before we proceed and you may cancel under
      section 8 if you do not accept the revised figure.
    </li>
    <li>Bank charges on international transfers are yours.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. What our price includes</h2>
  <p>Unless the quotation says otherwise, the price we quote covers:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Loading and lashing the vehicle into the ship</li>
    <li>UK customs export and clearance</li>
    <li>UK port handling charges</li>
    <li>DVLA documentation and export certification</li>
    <li>Export documentation, including the Bill of Lading</li>
    <li>Posting the original export documents from the UK by UPS or DHL</li>
    <li>Shipping the vehicle from the UK to the destination port</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. What it does not include</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Pre-export inspection, where the destination country requires one</li>
    <li>Marine insurance, which we can arrange on container shipments</li>
    <li>Collection of the vehicle in the UK, or delivery at the far end</li>
    <li>
      <strong>All import fees in the destination country</strong> — customs duty, VAT or its local equivalent,
      registration tax, port storage, demurrage and agent's fees
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This is the most important line in these terms, so it is worth stating plainly: <strong>our service ends at
    the UK quayside and the sea leg we have booked.</strong> What a vehicle costs to clear and register at the
    other end is set by that country's government, changes without notice, and is the importer's
    responsibility. Any figure we mention about destination duty is general background, not advice, and must be
    checked with a clearing agent in that country before you commit.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Your responsibilities</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Give us accurate information about the vehicle and the consignee. Customs entries are made from what you
      tell us, and a correction after filing can be expensive.
    </li>
    <li>
      Make sure the vehicle is yours to export, with no outstanding finance, and that it is not stolen, subject
      to a claim, or on an insurance write-off register you have not told us about.
    </li>
    <li>
      Present the vehicle in roadworthy, driveable condition for a RoRo sailing, with no more than a quarter
      tank of fuel and no warning lights that would stop it being driven aboard.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not leave personal effects in a vehicle shipped RoRo.</strong> Carriers require it to be empty.
      Anything left inside travels at your own risk and may be removed, and it can put the whole shipment in
      breach of the carrier's terms. If you want to ship possessions, use a container.
    </li>
    <li>
      Check what your destination country allows before you buy. Age limits, emissions standards and
      left-hand-drive rules vary and are your responsibility.
    </li>
    <li>Provide the identity and address documents we need for customs and anti-money-laundering checks.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>7. Transit, delays and risk</h2>
  <p>
    Once a vehicle is delivered into the carrier's custody, it travels under that carrier's own terms and the
    Bill of Lading. Sailing dates and arrival dates are estimates given by the carrier. Weather, port
    congestion, customs inspection, blank sailings and vessel rotation changes all move them, and none of that
    is within our control.
  </p>
  <p>
    We will keep you informed and do what we reasonably can to limit the effect of a delay, but we are not
    liable for loss caused by a sailing running late, being cancelled, or a vessel omitting a port. Where a
    vehicle is lost or damaged in transit, the claim lies against the carrier or the marine insurer under their
    terms, and we will help you bring it.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Cancellation</h2>
  <p>
    You may cancel before the vehicle is loaded, but you remain responsible for costs we have already incurred
    on your behalf — a purchase completed at your instruction, transport already carried out, a booking fee the
    carrier will not refund, storage, or customs work already filed. We will set those out and refund the
    balance.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you are a consumer contracting at a distance, you have a statutory right to cancel within 14 days of the
    contract being made. Where you have asked us to begin work during that period, you may be charged for what
    has been done up to the point you cancel. This does not affect your other legal rights.
  </p>
  <p>
    We may cancel if payment is not made when due, if the information you gave us proves materially wrong, or if
    exporting the vehicle would breach sanctions, export controls or any other law.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Our liability</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing in these terms limits our liability for death or personal injury caused by our negligence, for fraud
    or fraudulent misrepresentation, or for anything else that cannot lawfully be limited. For a consumer,
    nothing here affects your rights under the Consumer Rights Act 2015.
  </p>
  <p>
    Subject to that, and except where you are a consumer, our total liability arising out of a shipment is
    limited to the amount you paid us for it, and we are not liable for loss of profit, loss of use, loss of a
    resale, or other indirect or consequential loss.
  </p>

